      model: "The social thriller is a genre machine for building fear from social mechanics: the monster is society, or a specific mechanism within it, and the subtext IS the mechanism — the Armitages' admiration is literally the appetite; the Tethered are literally the people whose suffering funds surface life; Jean Jacket literally eats the spectacle-seekers. Peele's craft inherits from sketch comedy the engineered-surprise structure (setup, timed tension, release — the only variable being which involuntary sound the audience produces) and from Key & Peele an archive of social discomfort: code-switching, performance, the labor of reading a room. Across three films the machine is fed by a consistent toolkit: planted objects detonated late, survival-rule reversals (his Black protagonists survive by understanding the world's rules), animals as moral mirrors, screens and frames as capture/escape devices, and unexplained monsters — ambiguity as respect. The metaphors are built to be portable (the Sunken Place entering ordinary speech within weeks) because they do conceptual work language lacked shortcuts for; the theoretical lineage — Du Bois's double consciousness, Freud's uncanny, Wood's return of the repressed, Debord's spectacle, hooks's oppositional gaze — is delivered through clean genre mechanics, on the Serling principle that mass audiences accept moral allegory when the story machine is airtight.",
