const recursiveAiResilientSkillsTopics: readonly TopicCard[] = [
  {
    id: "ai-resilient-skills-full",
    title: "Recursive, AI-Resilient Skills: Learning the Loops That Learn",
    summary: "An English essay-lesson on the loops that learn: fixing the vocabulary first — AI-proof as a slightly dishonest phrase (almost no skill permanently immune; anyone promising never is making a bet not stating a fact) replaced by AI-resilient: a skill whose value holds up or rises as AI gets better, resting on the economists' substitute/complement distinction (a substitute competes with the machine — the better it gets, the less you are worth; a complement works with it — the better it gets, the more you are worth, as cheap spreadsheets made good financial analysts more valuable rather than less); recursive as the more interesting word — the bootstrapping compiler image (a compiler written in its own language compiling better versions of itself; each version builds a stronger successor), so a recursive skill practiced improves your ability to practice it and often your ability to learn other skills: output feeds back into input; the subject — skills that compound and become more valuable as AI becomes more capable, the highest-leverage investments right now because they pay interest on themselves at a rising rate; Part II why recursion matters more than content — two kinds of knowledge: content (syntax, tax rules, lab protocols — exactly what LLMs absorb best, existing in text, in bulk, on the internet, and depreciating as frameworks change, laws amend, protocols get replaced) versus process (how you figure things out, decide what matters, tell good work from bad — harder to write down, harder to train on, not expiring when content changes; strong process picks up new content quickly, content-only strands when it changes); recursive skills as process skills with a feedback loop behaving like compound interest rather than salary (same amount monthly vs interest on what you have already earned — small difference over a year, enormous over twenty), hence the most important career decisions rarely about what to learn next but about which loops to install; Part III the core recursive skills: (1) meta-learning — the root loop all others run on, understanding how your own learning works (spaced repetition, retrieval practice, interleaving, building things versus the methods that merely feel productive: rereading, highlighting, passively watching tutorials), the recursion direct (every time you learn something you also learn how you learned it — what worked, where you got stuck, what to do differently — making the next thing faster, giving more cycles, teaching more about learning; in ML terms you are not just training a model, you are tuning the optimizer), critical because the half-life of specific skills is shrinking (competent in a new domain in three weeks instead of six months as the durable edge over any fixed body of knowledge); (2) problem formulation — Kettering's a problem well stated is a problem half solved made truer than he imagined (AI extraordinary at solving well-specified problems, much weaker at which problem is worth solving, what the real constraints are, what the person asking actually needs versus asked for), the skill of turning a vague situation (sales are down; the code feels slow; the team seems unhappy) into a precise question — separating symptoms from causes, spotting hidden assumptions, choosing the right abstraction level, with the recursion being the template library: every well-framed problem solved gives a pattern recognizable in future situations (experienced engineers, doctors and investors do not think faster — they recognize the problem's shape sooner and search a much smaller space), and this being the skill that sets the ceiling on AI's usefulness to you (prompts only as good as your understanding of the problem); (3) judgment, verification, taste — the complexity-theory asymmetry that checking a solution is much easier than generating one (verifying a proof easier than discovering it; spotting a bad paragraph easier than writing a great one), with AI flipping the economics of generation (drafts, code, designs, analyses nearly free; what stays scarce is telling correct from subtly wrong from merely plausible), the GAN architecture made explicit (one network generates, another discriminates; AI is the generator and YOU are the discriminator, and the quality of the whole system is capped by the quality of the discriminator), judgment recursive because each evaluation — especially each wrong one learned from — sharpens the next; taste as judgment trained so thoroughly it runs fast and below awareness (the senior developer glancing at a pull request and saying something's off before she can say what), with the warning that taste cannot be built without ground truth (the code ran or crashed, the product sold or did not, the prediction came true or failed — judgment trained only on opinions drifts, judgment trained on outcomes compounds); (4) calibration — confidence matching accuracy (90% sure should mean right 90% of the time; most people badly overconfident, worse in an age of fluent machines because AI outputs sound equally confident whether right or wrong), trainable and measurable (forecasters' Brier score grading probabilistic predictions against outcomes; the practice: explicit predictions with probabilities, written down, checked later, adjusted), recursive in applying to itself (once you know you are overconfident about timelines but well calibrated on feasibility you can correct that bias, improving every future estimate — building an accurate model of your own mind's error patterns that cannot be outsourced because no one else has the data); (5) model-building and abstraction — the mental model as compressed representation (supply and demand, feedback loops, CAP theorem, natural selection, opportunity cost; expertise as good models plus the sense of which applies), the cognitive-science insight of chunking (experts do not hold more items in working memory — they hold bigger items: the chess master seeing a kingside attack structure where the beginner sees twelve pieces; each chunk a building block for larger chunks — recursion in its purest form, abstractions built from abstractions like functions composed of functions), the AI-resilience point that models let you reason across domains (AI can retrieve a model if you ask; knowing a supply-chain problem is secretly a queueing-theory problem, or an organizational conflict has the structure of a prisoner's dilemma, requires seeing the connection first — cross-domain synthesis remaining where human insight adds most); (6) explanation and teaching — Feynman's test (can you explain it simply; explaining exposes gaps you did not know you had — reach for the next step and find nothing there), the loop doubly recursive (teaching improves understanding which improves teaching; clear explanation builds reputation and trust, which bring better problems, collaborators, opportunities — more to understand and explain), and in an AI world explanation takes a new role: organizations drowning in machine-generated content, and the person who can make a decision legible to the people who must live with it does something a generated summary cannot fully replace because the explanation carries their credibility; (7) accountability, trust, relationships — the 1979 IBM training slide (a computer can never be held accountable, therefore a computer must never make a management decision; whatever you think of the second half, the first half is structural: societies run on someone being responsible — contracts, medical decisions, engineering sign-offs, leadership calls need a person with something at stake), trust as the most obviously recursive asset (reliability earns trust, trust earns responsibility, responsibility handled well earns more trust; compounding slowly, collapsing quickly — exactly why it is valuable; deeply local, living in specific relationships, not downloadable); (8) agency and problem selection — Hamming's You and Your Research question (what are the most important problems in your field and why aren't you working on them; the difference between good and great careers often less ability than choosing what to work on), with execution getting cheaper and direction becoming the bottleneck (AI can multiply output tenfold, but multiplying effort aimed at the wrong target gets you lost faster), agency as the disposition to decide what should exist and pursue it rather than waiting for assignment, recursive because every self-directed project teaches what you are good at, what the world rewards, where the unexplored opportunities are — improving the next choice; Part IV the three-question test for any skill (does it compound — the recursion test: does practicing it make future practice more effective or other skills easier; does AI make it more valuable — the complement test: as AI improves does the skill become a bottleneck or redundant; is it anchored in something AI lacks — the anchor test: stakes, accountability, embodied experience, local context, relationships, preferences about what should happen — a skill passing all three a strong investment; memorizing API syntax fails all three, debugging intuition built from years of tracing real failures passes all three); Part V the trap of outsourced recursion — AI can quietly break your recursive loops while appearing to help (if AI writes all your code you never develop debugging intuition; drafts all your arguments, never the ability to structure thought; evaluates your options, judgment never trained on outcomes), cognitive offloading as useful for routine work but applied to core skills amounting to paying someone to go to the gym for you (the work gets done, you do not get stronger), and the practical principle: use AI to extend your loops, not to replace them, with five concrete patterns (attempt first then compare — the gap between your answer and the AI's is where learning happens; use AI as adversary — find the flaws, argue the opposite side, generate breaking test cases, training your discriminator; demand explanations you can verify — outputs you could not check mean trusting an oracle rather than using a tool; keep a prediction log — write what you expect before asking or running, compare after, building calibration almost automatically; teach what you learn — if you cannot explain it, you have not absorbed it); Part VI the big picture — the Lindy effect (the longer something survived the longer it will likely keep surviving: 1970s programming languages outliving last year's frameworks), applied to skills (learning, framing problems, judging quality, knowing your uncertainty, building models, explaining clearly, earning trust, choosing direction — valuable as long as civilization, surviving printing press, industrialization, the computer, likely surviving this transition not because machines cannot touch them but because each automation wave made them more central); the one-sentence summary — invest in the loops, not just the outputs: content is what AI hands you, loops are what let you get better at everything including using AI; the first depreciates, the second compounds; in a world where generation is nearly free, the scarce resource is the mind that knows what to generate, how to judge it, and how to keep improving at both; and the exercise for the week — pick one recursive skill, design a tiny daily loop under ten minutes with a feedback mechanism from reality (a prediction you check, a solution you compare, an explanation you test on someone else), run it seven days, then ask the meta-question: what did I learn about how I learn this.",
    concepts: [],
    practicePrompt: "Run the week's exercise: pick one skill from the eight (meta-learning, formulation, judgment, calibration, models, teaching, trust, agency), design a daily loop under ten minutes with a reality-feedback mechanism (a prediction you check, a solution you compare, an explanation you test on someone), run it seven days, then write the meta-answer: what did you learn about how you learn this? Nothing was executed — the loop runs on your own calendar.",
    checkPrompt: "Without references: why AI-proof is dishonest and what AI-resilient fixes (substitute vs complement; the spreadsheet-analyst example); the bootstrapping compiler image and why recursive skills compound; content vs process (which LLMs absorb best, which depreciates); the eight core skills with their recursion mechanisms (meta-learning as optimizer tuning; problem formulation's template library and the prompt ceiling; the generator/discriminator asymmetry and taste-without-ground-truth drifting; calibration's Brier-score loop; chunking and cross-domain synthesis; teaching's double loop and credibility; the IBM slide and trust's compound-quick-collapse; Hamming's problem selection); the three-question test and its two test cases (API syntax vs debugging intuition); cognitive offloading and the gym-payment frame; the five AI-extension patterns (attempt first, adversary, verifiable explanations, prediction log, teach what you learn); and the Lindy effect plus invest-in-loops.",
    tier: 1,
    complexity: 4,
    references: [
      "Charles Kettering on problem formulation (attributed, mid-20th century)",
      "Richard Hamming, You and Your Research (1986)",
      "Richard Feynman on explanation as the test of understanding",
      "Nassim Taleb on the Lindy effect (Antifragile, 2012); Philip Tetlock & Dan Gardner, Superforecasting (2015) — calibration and the Brier score",
      "Cognitive science on chunking (Chase & Simon, 1973); Ian Goodfellow et al. on GANs (2014)",
      "IBM training slide, 1979 (a computer can never be held accountable)"
    ],
    lesson: {
      problem: "Career advice under AI anxiety oscillates between doomed resistance (skills the machine will never do) and resignation, while the actual question goes unasked: which skills make future learning and judgment cheaper — and which habits quietly let AI break them. Without the substitute/complement distinction and the recursion frame, people optimise content (what AI absorbs best and depreciates fastest) instead of loops.",
      model: "AI-resilient skills are complements, not substitutes: their value rises as AI improves because they sit at the bottlenecks generation cannot fill. The durable ones are recursive — practicing them improves the practice (meta-learning tunes the optimizer; formulation builds a template library; judgment compounds through evaluated errors; calibration models your own error patterns; chunks compose into bigger chunks; teaching loops understanding and credibility; trust compounds; agency improves project choice). The test has three questions: does it compound, does AI make it more valuable, is it anchored in something AI lacks (stakes, accountability, embodiment, context). The trap is outsourced recursion: cognitive offloading of core skills pays someone else to train for you — so the operating principle is to extend the loops with AI, never replace them.",
      mechanics: "Definitions: AI-resilient (value holds or rises) via substitutes vs complements; recursion via the bootstrapping compiler — output feeds back into input, interest on interest. Content vs process: content is what models absorb best and what depreciates (frameworks change, laws amend); process survives content churn and picks up new content quickly. The eight loops: (1) meta-learning — study your own learning (retrieval practice, interleaving, building vs rereading/highlighting/passive tutorials); each learning event also teaches how you learned it, tuning the optimizer — the edge is weeks-to-competence shrinking. (2) Problem formulation — turning vague situations into precise questions (symptoms vs causes, hidden assumptions, abstraction level); every well-framed problem becomes a recognizable shape; prompts are capped by your understanding of the problem. (3) Judgment/verification/taste — checking is easier than generating (the complexity-theory asymmetry), AI makes generation nearly free, so you are the discriminator in a human-plus-machine GAN and the system's ceiling is your discriminator's quality; each evaluation sharpens the next; taste runs fast below awareness but needs ground truth (outcomes, not opinions — judgment trained on opinions drifts). (4) Calibration — confidence matched to accuracy; measured with prediction logs and Brier scores; recursive self-application (knowing your bias patterns corrects them; no one else has your data). (5) Model-building — mental models as compressed representations; chunking (experts hold bigger items, not more items — a kingside attack structure vs twelve pieces); models enable cross-domain synthesis (supply chain as queueing theory; conflict as prisoner's dilemma — the connection must be seen before AI can be asked). (6) Explanation/teaching — Feynman's test exposing gaps; the double loop (understanding improves teaching, teaching improves understanding) plus the reputation loop; legibility that carries your credibility, which generated summaries lack. (7) Accountability/trust — the IBM slide's structural half (someone must be responsible: contracts, sign-offs, leadership calls); trust as the most recursive asset (reliability → responsibility → more trust; slow to compound, quick to collapse; local, not downloadable). (8) Agency/problem selection — Hamming's question; execution cheap, direction the bottleneck; each self-directed project teaches what you are good at and what the world rewards. The test: compound? complement? anchored? (API syntax fails all three; debugging intuition passes all three). The trap: AI writing all your code means no debugging intuition; drafting all arguments means no structured thought; evaluating all options means untrained judgment — paying someone to go to the gym for you. Extensions: attempt first then compare (the gap is where learning happens); AI as adversary (find flaws, argue the opposite, break the code — training your discriminator); demand verifiable explanations; keep a prediction log; teach what you learn. Lindy: what survived the printing press, industrialization and the computer — learning, framing, judging, calibrating, modeling, explaining, trusting, choosing — is positioned to survive this wave too, made more central by it.",
      pitfalls: [
        "Chasing AI-proof in the frontier-relative sense: almost no skill is permanently immune; the honest target is resilience — value that rises as the machine improves.",
        "Investing in content while ignoring process: syntax, tax rules and lab protocols are exactly what models absorb best and what depreciates under them.",
        "Confusing feeling productive with learning: rereading, highlighting and passive tutorials have weak transfer; retrieval, interleaving and building things are the actual loop.",
        "Training taste without ground truth: judgment fed only on opinions drifts — the code must run or crash, the product must sell or not, the prediction must come true or fail.",
        "Overtrusting fluent output: AI sounds equally confident whether right or wrong — calibration must be trained explicitly (predictions written down, checked, adjusted) or it decays.",
        "Outsourcing the core loop: AI writing all your code, drafting all arguments, or evaluating all options pays someone else to train for you — the work gets done, you do not get stronger.",
        "Using AI as an oracle: outputs you cannot evaluate mean trusting a black box — demand explanations you can verify, or you are not using a tool.",
        "Skipping the meta-question: without asking what this learning taught you about learning, the loop never closes and compounds never start."
      ],
      whenNot: "This is a career-and-learning frame, not a model-capabilities forecast: it makes no claim about which specific skills AI will automate next, and the three-question test evaluates skills for personal investment — it does not settle distributional questions (how many complement roles an economy keeps), nor does the Lindy argument guarantee survival of any particular craft."
    },
  },
];

