      model: "Dopamine is a setter of gains and thresholds, and it sits at both ends of the gut–brain loop without ever crossing between them: the molecule cannot pass the blood–brain barrier, so the body runs two dopamine compartments communicating only through nerves (vagus, sympathetic, spinal), immune messengers, hormones and microbes. In the gut dopamine is a restraining signal — a D2 brake on acetylcholine-driven motility, a D1 shield for the mucosa (bicarbonate, splanchnic flow), an immune damper that degrades the NLRP3 inflammasome. In the brain it is the currency of prediction error, salience and precision: how loud a gut sensation is allowed to be. IBS is a disorder of a loop whose calibration is off — mis-set gains and mis-weighted predictions — so one signal that works at nearly every node of that loop (gut wall, immune system, microbes, brain expectation) is the least famous but most promising thread in the field, while the better-proven serotonin runs the approved drugs.",
