    summary: "An English essay-lesson on recursive self-improvement as the first technology whose product — cognition — is also the input to its own construction: the lineage from I. J. Good 1965 (the ultraintelligent machine as the last invention, provided it is docile enough to tell us how to keep it under control) through Vinge 1993 (technological singularity as the point past which prediction breaks down) to Bostrom 2014 (optimization power over recalcitrance); the anatomy of the loop in three strengths — parameter-level (just learning, humans fix the algorithm and architecture), algorithmic (AutoML, and AlphaEvolve 2025 discovering matrix-multiplication algorithms while improving the data-center scheduling its successors run on) and research-level (the whole job of an AI scientist, the only form where the rate of improvement is itself subject to improvement); the object/meta distinction, then the mathematics: dI/dt = kI as exponential, kI² and above as vertical asymptote (the hard-takeoff skeleton and the literal source of the word singularity), Bostrom rate = optimization-power/recalcitrance, diminishing vs increasing returns, and the 2008 Hanson–Yudkowsky debate (Hanson: broad gradual distributed accelerations from history; Yudkowsky: the chimp–human hardware gap producing civilization as evidence for discontinuity; the empirical record since — smooth scaling laws favoring Hanson, extreme compute concentration supporting the concern); the four bottlenecks — compute (software-intelligence-explosion as the escape that likely hits a ceiling, months of training on physical timescales), verification (the evaluation problem as arguably the deepest barrier; AlphaZero self-play as the purest demonstrated RSI because chess supplies a perfect evaluator; benchmarks saturate and contaminate), data (STaR bootstrapping vs model collapse; synthetic data as ladder or treadmill depending on the ground-truth filter), and the Löbian obstacle (a formal system cannot prove its own soundness; self-trust as a warning rather than a wall; Vingean reflection on a successor you cannot predict); safety as two parts — goal drift under instrumental convergence (errors locked in and amplified, the improver improving its ability to defend whatever it wants) and loss of oversight (corrigibility as prerequisite, current lab frameworks naming automated AI R&D as a heightened-precaution threshold, the loop no longer needing a human in it); the present state honestly described — LLMs writing much of the code at frontier labs, RL from AI feedback, overnight agentic experiment loops: partial, human-mediated recursive improvement, accelerating; and the takeaway: not a sci-fi event but a change in the derivative — technologies always improved, this is the first whose rate of improvement can be improved by itself, with engineering to decide where between explosion and steep curve the truth lies.",
